; A 64-bit atomic load and a 64-bit atomic store.
;
; Both used to be refused: this host's Metal has no 64-bit atomic instruction, and
; a plain load of eight bytes can be torn by another lane writing the other half.
; They take the shape the pair updates already take, one thread for the whole
; grid, which walks every warp, and there the pair has a single reader and a
; single writer, so two 32-bit atomic loads are the 64-bit load and two 32-bit
; atomic stores are the 64-bit store.
;
; The arithmetic is order-free, so the two engines agree whatever order the lanes
; run in. Each lane loads A, takes `A - A` (which keeps the load live without
; making the answer depend on the buffer's initial contents), adds one, and stores
; that to B, so B ends holding 1 in both.
               OpCapability Kernel
               OpCapability Addresses
               OpCapability Int64
               OpCapability Int64Atomics
               OpMemoryModel Physical64 OpenCL
               OpEntryPoint Kernel %main "atom64rw"
               OpName %main "atom64rw"
      %void = OpTypeVoid
       %u32 = OpTypeInt 32 0
       %u64 = OpTypeInt 64 0
      %u64p = OpTypePointer CrossWorkgroup %u64
      %fnty = OpTypeFunction %void %u64p %u64p
       %scp = OpConstant %u32 1
       %sem = OpConstant %u32 0
       %one = OpConstant %u64 1
      %main = OpFunction %void None %fnty
        %a0 = OpFunctionParameter %u64p
        %a1 = OpFunctionParameter %u64p
     %entry = OpLabel
       %old = OpAtomicLoad %u64 %a0 %scp %sem
        %z  = OpISub %u64 %old %old
       %inc = OpIAdd %u64 %z %one
             OpAtomicStore %a1 %scp %sem %inc
             OpReturn
             OpFunctionEnd
